# Shell scripts must check out with LF even on Windows. Both scripts in
# tools/ are meant to be run by a Linux bash -- relay_admin.sh is documented
# as `ssh root@relay... 'bash -s' < tools/relay_admin.sh`, piping the working
# copy's bytes straight into a remote shell. With core.autocrlf=true (the
# default on this project's Windows checkout) those bytes would carry CRLF
# and bash fails on the carriage returns: `$'\r': command not found`.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Same story for the systemd units: they are copied verbatim onto the relay
# box, and systemd reads a CRLF "ExecStart=...\r" as a path that ends in a
# carriage return.
relayd/deploy/* text eol=lf
# Protocol replay fixtures are a wire format, not source: one JSON object per
# LF-terminated line, byte-identical on every host, and the same file a person
# exports from Advanced diagnostics feeds the replay command. A CRLF rewrite on
# a Windows checkout would change the bytes a fixture is supposed to pin, so the
# corpus is pinned to LF and its validator asserts the same thing.
*.jsonl text eol=lf
# Screenshot references and every other image are bytes, not text. Git's
# autodetection already treats them as binary, but this checkout runs with
# core.autocrlf=true, and an explicit rule is cheaper than discovering a
# line-ending rewrite inside a PNG the hard way.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.webp binary
